# SwiftScan OS — Cursor Rules (Bare-Metal C / ASM)

You are working on a freestanding Multiboot i386 kernel for a bi-optic POS
scanner appliance (barcode imager + load-cell scale + operator display).

## Hard constraints

- No hosted libc. Do not `#include <stdio.h>`, `<stdlib.h>`, `<string.h>`, etc.
- No floating point in freestanding paths unless soft-float is explicitly wired.
- No dynamic allocation unless a heap allocator already exists in-tree.
- Prefer explicit widths: `uint8_t`, `uint16_t`, `uint32_t` from local headers.
- Keep interrupts disabled until a real IDT/PIC path is introduced.
- Never assume Linux syscalls, POSIX, or ELF dynamic linking.

## Language & ABI

- Boot path: NASM, Multiboot1, 32-bit protected mode.
- Kernel & HAL: freestanding C (`-ffreestanding -fno-builtin -fno-stack-protector`).
- Link with `linker.ld`; load address is 1 MiB (`0x00100000`).
- Export symbols with C linkage from ASM (`global` / `extern`).
- Match calling convention: System V i386 (args on stack, EAX return).

## Memory & hardware

- Do not relocate sections without updating `linker.ld` and the Multiboot header.
- MMIO / port I/O only through HAL helpers (`outb`/`inb` or mapped bases).
- Scanner and scale drivers must tolerate missing hardware (QEMU stubs OK).
- Framebuffer code must handle both Multiboot graphics info and VGA text fallback.

## Style

- Small, reviewable modules under `src/boot`, `src/kernel`, `src/hal`, `src/ui`.
- Public APIs in `include/*.h`; keep drivers behind stable HAL entry points.
- Comments explain *why* (hardware quirks, Multiboot contracts), not narration.
- When adding features, update the Makefile objects list and keep `make run` working.

## Build / test expectation

- `make` must produce `build/swiftscan.bin` (and ISO when GRUB tools exist).
- Prefer QEMU (`make run`) over claiming physical hardware support.
- Do not commit build artifacts under `build/` or `iso/`.
